{"type":"message","id":"6d106b6f","parentId":"8a8468d5","timestamp":"2026-05-21T22:47:31.504Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me look at the gateway documentation about how slash commands work in channels, especially Feishu.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_9e50b362279a48f4afdd942b","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"grep -i \"slash\\|/status\\|command\" /app/docs/channels/ --include=\"*.md\" -r 2>/dev/null | head -30"},"partialArgs":"{\"command\": \"grep -i \\\"slash\\\\|/status\\\\|command\\\" /app/docs/channels/ --include=\\\"*.md\\\" -r 2>/dev/null | head -30\"}"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_6dfc386d70ed45a2a242ac3d","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"grep -i \"slash\\|/status\" /app/docs/config-channels.md 2>/dev/null | head -20"},"partialArgs":"{\"command\": \"grep -i \\\"slash\\\\|/status\\\" /app/docs/config-channels.md 2>/dev/null | head -20\"}"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"nvidia","model":"deepseek-ai/deepseek-v4-flash","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":0,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779403643223,"responseId":"5c12600a5a0d4cfdb4d777548566c0b9"}}
{"type":"message","id":"22a8bd14","parentId":"6d106b6f","timestamp":"2026-05-21T22:47:31.551Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_9e50b362279a48f4afdd942b","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:All `openclaw matrix` commands accept `--verbose` (full diagnostics), `--json` (machine-readable output), and `--account <id>` (multi-account setups). Output is concise by default with quiet internal SDK logging. The examples below show the canonical form; add the flags as needed.\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:The recovery key is sensitive - pipe it via stdin instead of passing it on the command line. Set `MATRIX_RECOVERY_KEY` (or `MATRIX_<ID>_RECOVERY_KEY` for a named account):\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:The command reports three states:\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:`verify bootstrap` is the repair and setup command for encrypted accounts. In order, it:\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:For lower-level lifecycle handling - typically while shadowing inbound requests from another client - these commands act on a specific request `<id>` (printed by `verify list` and `verify request`):\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:| Command                                    | Purpose                                                             |\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:Without `--account <id>`, Matrix CLI commands use the implicit default account. If you have multiple named accounts and have not set `channels.matrix.defaultAccount`, they will refuse to guess and ask you to choose. When E2EE is disabled or unavailable for a named account, errors point at that account's config key, for example `channels.matrix.accounts.assistant.encryption`.\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:    Replace `assistant` with the account ID from the failed command, or omit `--account` for the default account.\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:### Thread inheritance and slash commands\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:Both commands accept `--account <id>` for multi-account setups. The repair flow:\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:Fallback slash commands: `/approve <id> allow-once`, `/approve <id> allow-always`, `/approve <id> deny`.\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:Only resolved approvers can approve or deny. Channel delivery for exec approvals includes the command text - only enable `channel` or `both` in trusted rooms.\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:## Slash commands\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:Slash commands (`/new`, `/reset`, `/model`, `/focus`, `/unfocus`, `/agents`, `/session`, `/acp`, `/approve`, etc.) work directly in DMs. In rooms, OpenClaw also recognizes commands that are prefixed with the bot's own Matrix mention, so `@bot:server /new` triggers the command path without a custom mention regex. This keeps the bot responsive to the room-style `@mention /command` posts that Element and similar clients emit when a user tab-completes the bot before typing the command.\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:Authorization rules still apply: command senders must satisfy the same DM or room allowlist/owner policies as plain messages.\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:- Set `defaultAccount` to pick the named account that implicit routing, probing, and CLI commands prefer.\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:- If you have multiple named accounts and no default is selected, CLI commands refuse to guess - set `defaultAccount` or pass `--account <id>`.\n/app/docs/channels/signal.md:By default, Signal is allowed to write config updates triggered by `/config set|unset` (requires `commands.config: true`).\n/app/docs/channels/wechat.md:Use `openclaw-weixin` in CLI commands and config paths.\n/app/docs/channels/wechat.md:To add another WeChat account, run the same login command again. For multiple\n/app/docs/channels/pairing.md:If no command owner is configured yet, approving a DM pairing code also bootstraps\n/app/docs/channels/pairing.md:`commands.ownerAllowFrom` to the approved sender, such as `telegram:123456789`.\n/app/docs/channels/pairing.md:That gives first-time setups an explicit owner for privileged commands and exec\n/app/docs/channels/pairing.md:commands or control the bot in groups. First-owner bootstrap is separate config\n/app/docs/channels/pairing.md:state in `commands.ownerAllowFrom`, and group chat delivery still follows the\n/app/docs/channels/matrix-migration.md:5. Put the recovery key for the Matrix account you are repairing in an account-specific environment variable. For a single default account, `MATRIX_RECOVERY_KEY` is fine. For multiple accounts, use one variable per account, for example `MATRIX_RECOVERY_KEY_ASSISTANT`, and add `--account assistant` to the command.\n/app/docs/channels/matrix-migration.md:6. If OpenClaw tells you a recovery key is needed, run the command for the matching account:\n/app/docs/channels/matrix-migration.md:7. If this device is still unverified, run the command for the matching account:\n/app/docs/channels/matrix-migration.md:   and type `yes` only when they match. The command exits successfully only\n/app/docs/channels/matrix-migration.md:- What to do: verify which recovery key is correct before retrying any restore command."}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":33,"aggregated":"/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:All `openclaw matrix` commands accept `--verbose` (full diagnostics), `--json` (machine-readable output), and `--account <id>` (multi-account setups). Output is concise by default with quiet internal SDK logging. The examples below show the canonical form; add the flags as needed.\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:The recovery key is sensitive - pipe it via stdin instead of passing it on the command line. Set `MATRIX_RECOVERY_KEY` (or `MATRIX_<ID>_RECOVERY_KEY` for a named account):\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:The command reports three states:\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:`verify bootstrap` is the repair and setup command for encrypted accounts. In order, it:\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:For lower-level lifecycle handling - typically while shadowing inbound requests from another client - these commands act on a specific request `<id>` (printed by `verify list` and `verify request`):\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:| Command                                    | Purpose                                                             |\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:Without `--account <id>`, Matrix CLI commands use the implicit default account. If you have multiple named accounts and have not set `channels.matrix.defaultAccount`, they will refuse to guess and ask you to choose. When E2EE is disabled or unavailable for a named account, errors point at that account's config key, for example `channels.matrix.accounts.assistant.encryption`.\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:    Replace `assistant` with the account ID from the failed command, or omit `--account` for the default account.\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:### Thread inheritance and slash commands\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:Both commands accept `--account <id>` for multi-account setups. The repair flow:\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:Fallback slash commands: `/approve <id> allow-once`, `/approve <id> allow-always`, `/approve <id> deny`.\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:Only resolved approvers can approve or deny. Channel delivery for exec approvals includes the command text - only enable `channel` or `both` in trusted rooms.\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:## Slash commands\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:Slash commands (`/new`, `/reset`, `/model`, `/focus`, `/unfocus`, `/agents`, `/session`, `/acp`, `/approve`, etc.) work directly in DMs. In rooms, OpenClaw also recognizes commands that are prefixed with the bot's own Matrix mention, so `@bot:server /new` triggers the command path without a custom mention regex. This keeps the bot responsive to the room-style `@mention /command` posts that Element and similar clients emit when a user tab-completes the bot before typing the command.\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:Authorization rules still apply: command senders must satisfy the same DM or room allowlist/owner policies as plain messages.\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:- Set `defaultAccount` to pick the named account that implicit routing, probing, and CLI commands prefer.\n/app/docs/channels/matrix.md:- If you have multiple named accounts and no default is selected, CLI commands refuse to guess - set `defaultAccount` or pass `--account <id>`.\n/app/docs/channels/signal.md:By default, Signal is allowed to write config updates triggered by `/config set|unset` (requires `commands.config: true`).\n/app/docs/channels/wechat.md:Use `openclaw-weixin` in CLI commands and config paths.\n/app/docs/channels/wechat.md:To add another WeChat account, run the same login command again. For multiple\n/app/docs/channels/pairing.md:If no command owner is configured yet, approving a DM pairing code also bootstraps\n/app/docs/channels/pairing.md:`commands.ownerAllowFrom` to the approved sender, such as `telegram:123456789`.\n/app/docs/channels/pairing.md:That gives first-time setups an explicit owner for privileged commands and exec\n/app/docs/channels/pairing.md:commands or control the bot in groups. First-owner bootstrap is separate config\n/app/docs/channels/pairing.md:state in `commands.ownerAllowFrom`, and group chat delivery still follows the\n/app/docs/channels/matrix-migration.md:5. Put the recovery key for the Matrix account you are repairing in an account-specific environment variable. For a single default account, `MATRIX_RECOVERY_KEY` is fine. For multiple accounts, use one variable per account, for example `MATRIX_RECOVERY_KEY_ASSISTANT`, and add `--account assistant` to the command.\n/app/docs/channels/matrix-migration.md:6. If OpenClaw tells you a recovery key is needed, run the command for the matching account:\n/app/docs/channels/matrix-migration.md:7. If this device is still unverified, run the command for the matching account:\n/app/docs/channels/matrix-migration.md:   and type `yes` only when they match. The command exits successfully only\n/app/docs/channels/matrix-migration.md:- What to do: verify which recovery key is correct before retrying any restore command.","cwd":"/home/node/.openclaw/workspace-lucus"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1779403651547}}
